  "query_preview": "(This evening through Monday)...\nIssued at 703 PM EDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nSurface analysis early this afternoon shows high pressure over NY \nand PA while a frontal boundary was found over MN and SD, sagging \nSE. This was resulting in southerly surface flow across Indiana. A \nconvective cluster was found over IA and Nrn IL. GOES19 shows high \ncloud associated with this drifting SE into NW Indiana along with \nsct CU development across Central Indiana. Haze remained present in \nmost observations. Aloft the upper flow continued to be dominated by \nhigh pressure in place across the central Rockies, which was \nallowing a stream of Pacific moisture to flow across the NW CONUS \nacross the northern plains and diving southeastward toward the Ohio \nValley.\n\nTonight...Quiet weather is expected this evening as the upper wave \nover the upper midwest continues to approach. As daytime heating is \nlost, CU will dissipate this evening leading to partly cloudy skies, \nbut haze and high clouds may still linger.\n\nOvernight, the strong upper wave will dig southward, triggering \nshowers and storms as they pass across Central Indiana. Forecast \nsoundings suggest deep saturation overnight and pwats over 2 inches. \nCAPE appears relatively marginal while shear values remain high \nenough that a few severe storms cannot be ruled out. The main threat \nfrom any storms will be damaging straight line winds and heavy rain \nresulting in flooding. Diurnally, these showers and storms will not \nbe passing at favorable time of day for strong convection, so \noverall confidence is medium. HRRR shows area of showers and storms \nentering NW Central Indiana by 07-08Z before passing through the \nIndy metro between 10Z-13Z, and exiting Central Indiana by 14Z-15Z \nTuesday. HRRR continues to focus best forcing and storm organization \nover the northern and eastern parts of the forecast area, and will \nfocus best pops in those locations.\n\nTuesday afternoon through Wednesday, strong Canadian high pressure \nover the northern plains will begin to build east into the Great \nLakes and Ohio Valley. This will result in cooler northerly flow \nacross Indiana on Tuesday afternoon through Wednesday night. \nForecast soundings show strong subsidence and a dry column, which \nshould result in a mostly clear nights and partly cloudy days, with \nhighs only in the middle 70s on Wednesday.\n\nThursday through Monday...\n\nDry and cooler weather is mainly expected until Sunday and Monday. \nModels suggest strong ridging developing aloft over the western \nCONUS on Thursday, persisting through Sunday. This will result in \nleeside NW flow spilling from the northern Plains and upper midwest \nand into the Ohio Valley. At the moment, little in the way of \nforcing dynamics appear to pass until Sunday and Monday. Best \nforcing looks to pass on Monday afternoon at this point, but \nuncertainty with timing at this point will remain high.\n\nOtherwise, we will look for a large surface high pressure system \nlingering across Indiana on Thursday through Sunday, with subsidence \nin place from the flow aloft. This Canadian air mass will be much \ncooler, allowing below normal temperatures as highs will only reach \nthe upper 70s and lower 80s through Saturday.\n\nWarmer and more humid southerly flow will return on Sunday and \nMonday, adding moisture for the potential of storms at that time as \nthat previously discussed upper wave approaches on Monday.",
